qnx# slay fs-9p   # unmount; fs-9p prints its statistics
fs-9p: signal 15, unmounting /mnt/host (27 9P requests, 0 Rlerror, 27 interrupts, 0 found after unmask, 0 reaped by waiters, 0 lost, at most 1 requests in flight)
fs-9p: priorities: highest waiting client 40; interrupt thread base 21, raised 24 times, highest 40; set below the base 1 times, lowest 10; 0 priority changes failed

### Summary (counts and priorities from the output above)
reader at priority 40, interrupt thread while the read waited (pidin): 21 with -f, 40 by default
with 8 loops at priority 30: 24 of 27 9P requests were completed by the waiting server thread's fallback wait instead of the interrupt thread with -f (highest interrupt-thread priority 21); 0 of 27 by default (highest 40)
fallback wait: a server thread that waits for the device looks at the ring itself every 200 ms, in case the interrupt thread did not run (a constant in src/virtq.c, not a measurement); the counts say how often that check, not the interrupt thread, completed a request

